AnnotationAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_ATC_code X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone "C01BD07"^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone "dronedarone"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Drug X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:hasClass X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:antiarrhythmics_class_III)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_mechanism X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:MechanismOfDronedarone)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_mechanism X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedarone X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT)

# Individual: X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT (dronedarone exacerbating Type II AIT)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT "dronedarone exacerbating Type II AIT"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:hyperthyroidism)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_Dronedarone_hyperthyroidism_decreased_TSH_4th:dronedaroneExacerbatingTypeIIAIT "The most likely diagnosis for the patients in the reports who developed hyperthyroidism on amiodarone is Type II AIT. This may also be true for those who first developed hyperthyroidism on dronedarone. Supporting evidence comes from reports with laboratory results showing an elevated T4 and markedly suppressed TSH. The TSH was increased in one patient with hyperthyroidism (report 5 in Table 2) suggesting an inhibition of monodeiodination as the mechanism rather than AIT in this one report.
Type II AIT is slow to remit after amiodarone is withdrawn and may in fact be exacerbated by withdrawal as the protection against the arrhythmias of hyperthyroidism is removed.
It is therefore possible that patients who had developed hyperthyroidism on amiodarone were still experiencing fluctuating disease while taking dronedarone. Several patients developed a recurrence of atrial fibrillation. This may have been due to dronedarone-induced hyperthyroidism but recurrence of previous thyroid-related atrial arrhythmias may have occurred if dronedarone was less effective than amiodarone at controlling these."^^xsd:string)

