ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ADRs_of_everolimus X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:Ref.11)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ADRs_of_everolimus "Apart from the official labelling, many of the known adverse reactions of everolimus come from its extensive use in transplantation patients and solid organ malignancy, notably renal cell carcinoma. Oral treatment with everolimus 10 mg daily or 50 mg once weekly resulted in adverse effects including stomatitis, rash, fatigue, headache, anorexia, diarrhoea, neutropenia, hyperglycaemia, hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, mild leukocytopenia, and thrombocytopenia. Other toxicities include infectious and non-infectious pneumonitis.11\""^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus (Clinical trials for everolimus)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus "Clinical trials for everolimus"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Clinical_trial_information X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:everolimus)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:Ref.8)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:Ref.9)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:ClinicalTrialsForEverolimus "A large number of published and ongoing clinical studies have recently shown promising antitumor activity of everolimus against different tumour types such as breast cancer, advanced gastrointestinal cancer, hepatocellular carcinoma, and lymphoma, when used as monotherapy or in combination with vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) and epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) inhibitors.8,9"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:DiscussionAndConclusion (Discussion and conclusion)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:DiscussionAndConclusion "Discussion and conclusion"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Conclusion X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:DiscussionAndConclusion)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Discussion X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:DiscussionAndConclusion)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_everolimus_and_serious_gastrointestinal_disorders_3rd:DiscussionAndConclusion "VigiBase reports of selected serious GI adverse reactions (GI haemorrhages and ulcers) associated with everolimus allow us to suspect a possible connection between the drug and the development or worsening of GI toxicity.
We would like to stress that patients' typology is various and ranges from transplantation to oncology. Many of the patients are taking other drugs and potential and individual risk factors for the development of GI toxicity cannot be gathered from the reports. However, the proportion of fatal outcomes is high in patients who experienced the selected GI disorders in association with everolimus treatment. The prospective of an increase in use of everolimus in different clinical settings, also in combination with other drugs such as bevacizumab and sorafenib, makes the issue of utility of this drug. Vigilance is therefore necessary for identifying any emerging toxicity which has not been reported yet."^^xsd:string)

