# Individual: X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome (proposed mechanism of ondasetron triggering serotonine syndrome)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome "proposed mechanism of ondasetron triggering serotonine syndrome"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:serotoninSyndrome)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:Ref.10)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_ondasetrone_3rd:proposedMechanismOfOndasetronTriggeringSerotonineSyndrome "A mechanism for a suggested increased vulnerability to serotonin syndrome when concomitantly using 5-HT3 antagonists and other serotonergic drugs might be that blocking of the 5-HT3 receptor subtype, and at the same time increasing the levels of serotonin, results in excessive serotonin to other serotonin receptor subtypes (including 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A).7,10 A pharmacokinetic interaction might also be considered, as ondansetron is metabolized by cytochrome P-450 enzymes; CYP3A4, CYP2D6 and CYP1A21."^^xsd:string)

