# Individual: X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:IntroductionContent "Mometasone is a synthetic corticosteroid with anti- inflammatory, antipruritic and vasoconstrictive properties.1 It is available in three dosage forms: topical, nasal spray and dry powder inhalation.
The topical form is presented as a cream or lotion and is indicated for the short term relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid responsive dermatoses, such as psoriasis and atopic dermatitis. Common adverse effects (AEs) of the topical preparations include skin reactions such as pruritus, burning, tingling, signs of skin atrophy and acneiform reactions.1 Other local reactions have also been reported less commonly. The nasal spray is available for treatment of allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps and rhinosinusitis. Systemic absorption is possible from the nasal spray although bioavailability is regarded as low. Common AEs of the nasal preparation include headache, epistaxis and nasal reactions.
Other AEs include rare hypersensitivity reactions and disturbances of taste and smell.2 The dry powder inhalation is available for treatment of asthma, and oral candidiasis is a very common AE. Other common AEs include pharyngitis, headache and dysphonia. There is also potential for hypersensitivity reactions.3
Arrhythmia refers to disturbances in normal cardiac rhythm. It includes a large and heterogeneous group of conditions which arise from abnormal electrical activity in the heart. Arrhythmias can range from minor, common \"skipped beats\" to life-threatening conditions. In general, arrhythmia can refer to disturbances in rate or rhythm but simple disturbances in rate such as bradychardia or tachycardia would usually be coded as such. Arrhythmias may be caused by many different factors including coronary artery disease, electrolyte imbalance, heart muscle disorders and as indicated above, may occur in \"normal, healthy\" hearts.4 A wide range of drugs has been implicated in the development of arrhythmias including antihistamines, sympathomimetics, methylxanthines, anticholinergic drugs, antiarrhythmic drugs and a variety of other drugs including cisapride and erythromycin.5"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling (Product info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling "Product info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Warning_Information X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:mometasone)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Labelling "The product literature for mometasone does not refer to arrhythmia. As noted above, bioavailability is regarded as low from the nasal spray which has been implicated with the majority of the reports. The product information also notes that systemic effects were not detected in adults, adolescents or children following the administration of mometasone aqueous nasal spray.2"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.10)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.11)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.7)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.8)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.9)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:LiteratureAndLabelling "The product literature for mometasone does not refer to arrhythmia. As noted above, bioavailability is regarded as low from the nasal spray which has been implicated with the majority of the reports.
The product information also notes that systemic effects were not detected in adults, adolescents or children following the administration of mometasone aqueous nasal spray.2 Oteri and colleagues reported the development of a paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (AF) with fast ventricular response after the administration of a different inhaled corticosteroid, fluticasone propionate, to a 15 year old boy, which resolved after discontinuation of the drug. The patient had a history of allergic asthma and had been treated for several years with cetirizine and inhaled mometasone to treat seasonal allergic rhinitis.7 The authors noted that corticosteroid-induced AF had previously been reported in three case control studies.8-10 Huerta et al. reported a twofold increased risk of AF among users of oral corticosteroids compared with nonusers.
Conversely, no increased risk was found among users of inhaled corticosteroids. The results of a large population-based case control study, conducted in Denmark, showed an almost doubled risk of AF or flutter associated with systemic corticosteroid use, with respect to nonuse, in patients with and without chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or asthma and cardiovascular diseases.9 Van der Hooft also reported an increase in the risk of AF among users of systemic corticosteroids in a study which involved 385 cases of AF from the Netherlands.10 It has been postulated that high doses of corticosteroids mediate potassium efflux via a direct effect on the cell membrane, which may induce arrhythmogenesis.11"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:MechanismOfMometasone (Mechanism of mometasone)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:MechanismOfMometasone "Mechanism of mometasone"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:MechanismOfMometasone)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:MechanismOfMometasone X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:Ref.1)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2012_m_mometasone_and_arrhythmia_4rth:MechanismOfMometasone "Mometasone is a synthetic corticosteroid with anti- inflammatory, antipruritic and vasoconstrictive properties.1"^^xsd:string)

