# Individual: X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport (Australian Public Assessment Report)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport "Australian Public Assessment Report"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:abiraterone)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:Ref.4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:AustralianPublicAssessmentReport "The Australian Public Assessment Report does not mention thrombocytopenia as an adverse effect of abiraterone, although on page 88 it is stated that \"(in study COU-AA-301). No other haematologic Grade 2, 3 or 4 abnormality (i.e. neutrophils, platelets, WBC) during treatment occurred in greater than 5% of subjects in the abiraterone acetate group or in greater than 3% of subjects in the placebo group\".4"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:ConclusionContent (Conclusion content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:ConclusionContent "Conclusion content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Conclusion X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:ConclusionContent)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:ConclusionContent X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:Ref.12)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:ConclusionContent "In most of the ICSRs entered into VigiBase, the temporal relationship between abiraterone and thrombocytopenia suggests a possible causal relationship. Platelets have a circulating lifespan of around 10 days, and about one third of the platelets are sequestered in the spleen at any time. Approximately 100 x 109 platelets must be released from mature megakaryocytes into the circulation each day in order to maintain a normal platelet count.20 In the ICSRs, abiraterone treatment durations range from 18 days to four months. The temporal relationship between the start of abiraterone therapy and reported thrombocytopenia would suggest a process affecting platelet production, more prolonged than the mechanism of immune DIT. In the literature, an observational study12 reports two cases of thrombocytopenia (5%) in 39 patients treated with abiraterone and this combined with the analysis of reports in VigiBase, would suggest that abiraterone might be associated with thrombocytopenia, and requires further investigation."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology (DIT pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology "DIT pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:thrombocytopenia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:Ref.11)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:Ref.7)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:Ref.8)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_abiraterone_6th_2013:DIT_pathophysiology "Drug-induced thrombocytopenia (DIT) is a relatively common adverse drug reaction; when severe, its consequences may be serious.7,8 Two main pathways for DIT have been described: a decrease in platelet production — mainly because of bone marrow toxicity, i.e. a non-immune mechanism; and an increase in platelet destruction, predominantly caused by immune mechanisms but also by non-immune mechanisms.7,11"^^xsd:string)

