# Individual: X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfFluoxetine (Mechanism of Fluoxetine)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfFluoxetine "Mechanism of Fluoxetine"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfFluoxetine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfFluoxetine X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfFluoxetine "Fluoxetine is a Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitor (SSRI) used for major depressive illness, obsessive-compulsive disorder and bulimia nervosa.3"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI (Mechanism of ototoxicity by SSRI)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI "Mechanism of ototoxicity by SSRI"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2013_3_fluoxetine_deafness#Hearing_impairment_(SMQ)>)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:Ref.5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_fluoxetine_and_deafness_2013_3rd:MechanismOfOtotoxicityBySSRI "Serotonin is an important neurotransmitter in the auditory system, with both excitatory and inhibitory actions in different levels of the processing of sensory information of the acoustic signal. It has been hypothesized that the primary functions of the 5-HT system in the brain are to facilitate motor output and concurrently inhibit sensory information processing.4 The location of serotonin receptors in the cochlear nucleus and the inferior colliculus of the brainstem is well established, and it is postulated that serotonin may have a modulatory effect in the processing of the acoustic signal producing a tonic inhibition of the acoustic pathways.5"^^xsd:string)

