# Individual: X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PharmacokineticProfileOfAgomelatine (Pharmacokinetic profile of agomelatine)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PharmacokineticProfileOfAgomelatine "Pharmacokinetic profile of agomelatine"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PharmacokineticProfileOfAgomelatine)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PharmacokineticProfileOfAgomelatine "In in vitro assays, CYP1A2 was responsible for the major metabolism of agomelatine, whereas CYP2C9 and CYP2C19 were found likely to be of potential relevance only at higher agomelatine concentrations. The concomitant use of potent CYP1A2 inhibitors (e.g. fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin) is contraindicated."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine (Potential pharmacokinetic interaction for lansoprazol-agomelatine-escitalopram-irbesartan)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine "Potential pharmacokinetic interaction for lansoprazol-agomelatine-escitalopram-irbesartan"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.20)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:PotentialPharmacokineticInteractionForlansoprazol-agomelatine "Concerning pharmacokinetic interactions, lanzoprazole as well as all proton-pump inhibitors (PPIs) may inhibit CYP2C19 which metabolizes escitalopram20 (together with CYP2D6) and also plays a minor role in agomelatine’s metabolism.1,2,3,4 Irbesartan has a modest affinity for CYP1A2, suggesting that the theoretical potential for drug interactions is likely to be quite low for the main CYP metabolizing agomelatine, but it has been shown to inhibit tolbutamide methylhydroxylation more potently, suggesting that it could play a role in inhibiting CYP2C9 agomelatine metabolism."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies (Premarketing studies)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies "Premarketing studies"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:qtProlonged)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:agomelatine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Ref.4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_agomelatine_and_QT_prolonged_4th:Premarketing_studies "The effects of agomelatine on QT interval were evaluated in two specific premarketing studies. The first study included only 13 subjects and agomelatine did not seem to cause any clinically meaningful changes in cardiac depolarization. The second study was conducted in response to regulatory requirements and included 56 subjects (28 males and 28 females). The results showed that single doses of agomelatine 50 mg and 400 mg fulfilled the demands set out in the CHMP/ICH/2/04 “Note for guidance on the clinical evaluation of QT/ QTc interval prolongation and proarrhythmic potential for non-antiarrhythmic drugs”.2,4"^^xsd:string)

