# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_1_info (Case 1 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_1_info "Case 1 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_1_info)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_1_info X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Ref.12)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_1_info "The first concerns a 74 year old man from the US with a history of major depressive disorder who was brought to the emergency department for odd behaviour. He had started taking mirtazapine four months earlier and had been using lisinopril for two years. Three months prior to the incident he had had a dose increase of mirtazapine from 30 to 45 mg per day and lisinopril from 10 to 30 mg per day. The man was diagnosed with rhabdomyolysis and both drugs were discontinued. Other possible confounders were ruled out and lisinopril was reinstated with a negative rechallenge. The authors found a causative relationship between mirtazapine and rhabdomyolysis.12"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_2_info (Case 2 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_2_info "Case 2 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_2_info)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_2_info X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Ref.10)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_2_info "The second case concerns an overdose where a 40 year old man from Australia had been admitted to the emergency department for attempted suicide. He had taken 1.8 g of mirtazapine together with two litres of alcohol and developed rhabdomyolysis. Although the dose is not exactly the same as that reported in VigiBase, it is believed to concern the same event. The author suggests that the rhabdomyolysis may have been caused by mirtazapine.10"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_3_info (Case 3 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_3_info "Case 3 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_3_info)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_3_info X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Ref.13)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_3_info "In the third case a 40 year old man from Germany taking risperidone (8 mg daily) and biperiden (2 mg daily) after having developed a syndrome consistent with schizophrenia, was given mirtazapine (45 mg daily) for treatment of a following episode of major depression. When treatment failed, the mirtazapine dose was increased to 60 mg daily and risperidone reduced to 3 mg daily whereafter the patient improved. Six weeks after starting this combination therapy, the patient was admitted to hospital and diagnosed with pulmonary embolism and rhabdomyolysis. Mirtazapine and risperidone were withdrawn and replaced and after receiving medical therapy the patient recovered. The authors suggest the causal relationship to be likely for the psychotropic medications and the adverse events after having ruled out other confounders.13"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_4_info (Case 4 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_4_info "Case 4 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_4_info)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_4_info X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Ref.11)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:Case_4_info "The final case describes a neonate being delivered by emergency caesarean section in the 36th week of pregnancy after the mother had attempted suicide. The mother had overdosed on mirtazapine and venlafaxine 11 hours prior to this incident. The newborn had to be resuscitated and experienced seizures and rhabdomyolysis. Blood samples showed extremely high concentrations of the two compounds. Both mother and child survived.11"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:ConclusionContent (Conclusion content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:ConclusionContent "Conclusion content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Conclusion X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:ConclusionContent)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:ConclusionContent "The reports found in VigiBase together with the added information from the cases described in literature suggest that there is a positive causal relationship between mirtazapine and rhabdomyolysis that should be investigated further."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:DiscussionContent (Discussion content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:DiscussionContent "Discussion content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Discussion X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:DiscussionContent)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_Mirtazapine_and_rhabdomyolysis_5th:DiscussionContent "Rhabdomyolysis is a serious ADR and may be due to a number of causes. In the cases assessed in this analysis there are several confounders, such as co-reported drugs known to cause rhabdomyolysis, alcohol intoxication and possible infection. Rhabdomyolysis may be secondary to muscle rigidity seen in patients with serotonin syndrome or neuroleptic malignant syndrome.
Serotonin syndrome was listed in three cases and neuroleptic malignant syndrome was listed in one case. Other co-reported terms of interest included hypertonia, convulsions, involuntary muscle contractions and extrapyramidal disorders which all might, theoretically and if severe enough, have contributed to the rhabdomyolysis.
Data on causality assessment, co-morbidities and de-and re-challenge was extremely limited. In the 27 cases where information on causality assessment was given, 21 were graded as possible, three were not (yet) assessed, two were probable (one according to narrative information) and one was recorded as unknown. 17 reports provided information on co-morbidities, a few with possible confounders such as alcoholism and high cholesterol (statin use). De-challenge information was provided on 30 of the ICSRs; the drug was withdrawn in 26 cases and in eight of these it was mentioned that the reaction abated. Only one positive re-challenge was reported. Outcome was stated on 91% (43/47) of the ICSRs. 31 patients had recovered or were recovering, two of these with sequelae, three had not recovered at the time of reporting, one patient died and in eight cases the outcome was unknown"^^xsd:string)

