# Individual: X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2013_m_baclofen_and_renal_failure_6th:IntroductionContent "The drug-ADR combination of baclofen and renal failure was highlighted during testing of a new quantitative method for detecting potential signals at the UMC in 2012.1 As of 15 November 2012, 67 Individual Case Safety Reports (ICSRs) had been entered into the WHO Global ICSR Database, VigiBase™, raising a possible relationship between baclofen use and the occurrence of renal failure. The drug is mostly used in treating chronic spasticity, as typically associated with spinal cord damage and multiple sclerosis. Labelled adverse effects include hypotension and cardiovascular depression. Kidney failure is labelled in the US FDA Label for the injection but not for the oral formulation and renal failure is not labelled in the UK Summary of Product Characteristics (SPC).2,3"^^xsd:string)

