# Individual: X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PotentialMolecularMechanismOfADRByIbuprofen (Potential molecular mechanism of ADR by Ibuprofen)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PotentialMolecularMechanismOfADRByIbuprofen "Potential molecular mechanism of ADR by Ibuprofen"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PotentialMolecularMechanismOfADRByIbuprofen)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PotentialMolecularMechanismOfADRByIbuprofen X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:ErectileDysfunction)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PotentialMolecularMechanismOfADRByIbuprofen "Ibuprofen, like other NSAIDs, has the ability to inhibit cyclooxygenase which in turn reduces the prostaglandin synthesis. Prostaglandins are hormone-like substances involved in different processes including penis erection. Some of these substances have been used to treat erectile dysfunction. In theory, the inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis could account for erectile dysfunction."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies (Preclinical rat studies)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies "Preclinical rat studies"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001197 X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:ErectileDysfunction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:indomethacin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:Ref.8)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_Ibuprofen_2014_1st:PreclinicalRatStudies "In preclinical rat studies, single-dose administration of indomethacin significantly  reduced erectile responses to electrical stimulation at all frequencies tested in a dose-response method. Longer treatment with indomethacin completely abolished erectile responses at low frequencies and significantly reduced intracavernosal pressure at high frequencies, while diclofenac reduced erectile responses only at low frequencies."^^xsd:string)

