DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_parathyroid_hormone_and_myocardial_ischaemia_1st:MechanismOfPTH "Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is the most important endocrine regulator of calcium and phosphorus concentration in extracellular fluid. This hormone is secreted from cells of the parathyroid glands and finds its major target cells in bone and kidney.1 Physiological actions of PTH include stimulation of bone formation by direct effects on bone forming cells (osteoblasts) indirectly increasing the intestinal absorption of calcium and the tubular reabsorption of calcium and excretion of phosphate by the kidney. The therapeutic product is recombinant human PTH which is identical to the full-length native 84-amino acid polypeptide."^^xsd:string)

