# Individual: X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Mechanism_of_Tocilizumab (Mechanism of Tocilizumab)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Mechanism_of_Tocilizumab "Mechanism of Tocilizumab"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Mechanism_of_Tocilizumab)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Mechanism_of_Tocilizumab X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:ref5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Mechanism_of_Tocilizumab "Tocilizumab is a humanised anti-human interleukin (IL)-6 receptor antibody. It competes for membrane- bound and soluble forms of human IL- 6 receptor, thereby inhibiting the binding of this native inflammatory cytokine to its receptor and interfering with the cytokine’s effects. This cytokine has the ability to activate T cells, B cells, macrophages and osteoclasts.  Increased synovial fluid IL-6 levels correlate with disease activity in rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and circulating levels are elevated in children with systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA). Its approved indications include JIA and RA.5"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab (Proposed mechanism for psoriasis due to tocilizumab)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab "Proposed mechanism for psoriasis due to tocilizumab"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:psoriasis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:ref10)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:ref13)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_tocilizumab_2nd:Proposed_mechanism_for_psoriasis_due_to_tocilizumab "The mechanism whereby TNF inhibitors and tocilizumab might induce or aggravate psoriasis is unknown. Clearly this is a paradoxical reaction as these medicines inhibit the actions of pro- inflammatory cytokines. IL-6 appears to have a role in maintaining inflammation in psoriasis which includes promoting TNF and other cytokine production from T helper 17 cells which are important mediators in autoimmune diseases and host defence against extracellular pathogens.10 However, as there is evidence for abatacept and limited evidence for rituximab and anakinra causing psoriasis and these are biologic anti- rheumatic agents with various effects on the immune system,13 a specific mechanism related to TNF and IL-6 inhibition is less likely. Cytokine imbalance has been proposed as a possible mechanism.10"^^xsd:string)

