ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.15)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling "Neither the European Medicines Agency (EMA) Summary of Products Characteristis (SPC) nor US FDA labels indicate pancreatitis as a potential adverse reaction; moreover, there was no mention of either increased amylase or increased lipase enzymes. Gamma-glutamyltransferases, potential indicators of pancreatic inflammation, but more commonly associated with hepatobiliary problems, were found to be increased in phase III studies.3,14 There is one case report in the literature of a 49- year-old male with stage IV melanoma who developed pancreatitis two weeks after starting treatment with vemurafenib. He recovered following withdrawal of the drug, but pancreatitis recurred on rechallenge. His symptoms disappeared after permanent discontinuation of vemurafenib. All other possible causes were ruled out.15"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib (Mechanism of AE by vemurafenib)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib "Mechanism of AE by vemurafenib"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:pancreatitis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.17)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.18)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.19)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAEByVemurafenib "Secondly, the interruption of the RAS-RAF-MEK- ERK cascade by vemurafenib results in the activation of caspase-9; this enzyme has been found to promote apoptosis.17 Caspase-induced apoptosis has been pro- posed as one of the pathophysiological mechanisms for acute pancreatitis.7 Alternatively, low dose vemurafenib can result in paradoxical induction of ERK,18 which could activate TNF-α dependant transcription factors17 such as NF-κB that may be linked to acinar cell apo- ptosis and ultimately acute pancreatitis,.7,19"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction (Mechanism of vemurafenib)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction "Mechanism of vemurafenib"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:MechanismOfAction "Vemurafenib (Zelboraf®), a serine threonine protein kinase inhibitor, received FDA approval in August 2011 for use in the treatment of metastatic or unresectable malignant melanoma that is positive for the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug is active in the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK pathway, which is involved in the regulation of cell proliferation, survival, differentiation and apoptosis. Vemurafenib competes with adenosine-triphosphate (ATP) to reversibly and selectively inhibit the V600E mutated BRAF kinase, by preventing its phosphorylation mediated activation.1  The drug is administered orally as 240 mg tablets,2 usually 3-8 tablets per day. Vemurafenib is metabolized by CYP3A4 and mostly excreted in the faeces (94%), up to 5% of its metabolites can be found in plasma.3"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Pathogeneis_of_pancreatitis (Pathogeneis of pancreatitis)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Pathogeneis_of_pancreatitis "Pathogeneis of pancreatitis"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Pathogeneis_of_pancreatitis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Pathogeneis_of_pancreatitis X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:pancreatitis)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_pancreatitis_2014_2nd:Pathogeneis_of_pancreatitis "It is accepted that the initial inflammatory response involves the destruction of acinar cells through apoptotic or necrotic mechanisms with consequent leakage of pancreatic digestive enzymes, effectively leading to an autodigestion of the organ."^^xsd:string)

