# Individual: X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:IntroductionContent "Vemurafenib is a low molecular weight, orally available, inhibitor of BRAF serine-threonine kinase (a member of the Raf kinase family of growth signal transduction protein kinases). Mutations in the BRAF gene which substitute the valine at amino acid position 600 result in constitutively activated BRAF proteins, which can cause cell proliferation in the absence of growth factors that would normally be required for proliferation.1 Before taking vemurafenib, patients must have BRAF V600 mutation-positive tumour status confirmed by a validated test. It is one of the medications widely known as tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) but should be referred to as protein kinase inhibitors because although the earliest drugs in this class were TKIs, they can now be further categorized based upon the amino acid that they phosphorylate:  serine,  threonine or tyrosine. Vemurafenib is indicated in monotherapy for the treatment of adult patients with BRAF V600 mutation-positive unresectable or metastatic melanoma. The most common adverse drug reactions (ADR) reported with vemurafenib (>30%) reported with vemurafenib include arthralgia, fatigue, rash, photosensitivity reaction, nausea, alopecia and pruritus. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CuSCC) was very commonly reported and was most commonly treated by local excision. There are warnings in the product information concerning the possible occurrence of severe hypersensitivity and dermatological reactions, QT prolongation, serious ophthalmologic reactions, new primary melanomas, liver laboratory abnormalities and mild to severe photosensitivity.1
Renal failure is a reduction or suppression of the excretory function of the kidney, characterized by a decrease in creatinine clearance.2  Renal failure is demonstrated by an increase in serum creatinine above 150 mmol/L, or reduction in creatinine clearance below 50 mL/ min per 1.73 m2 of body surface. Age, sex, weight and whether the patient is pregnant should be considered in determining abnormality of levels of serum creatinine or creatinine clearance. The presenting symptoms of drug-induced renal failure are the same as those for renal failure due to other causes. Drugs can cause renal failure, which may be acute (developing within hours or days), subacute (within weeks), or chronic (within months or years); they may also exacerbate pre- existing renal failure. Whenever possible in reporting a suspected adverse drug reaction, the pre-existing level of renal function should be included in addition to the actual change in function.2
The cause of acute renal failure (ARF) can be characterised based on where the injury has occurred.3 Pre-renal causes include hypovolaemia, dehydration due to vomiting, diarrhoea, sweating or fever, poor intake of  fluids, medications such as diuretic’s and abnormal blood flow due to obstruction. Renal causes include sepsis, medications such as NSAIDs, antibiotics such as aminoglycosides, lithium and iodine, rhabdomyolysis, multiple myeloma and glomerulonephritis. Post-renal causes include obstruction of the bladder or the ureters, prostatic hypertrophy or prostate cancer, abdominal tumours or kidney stones. The most common causes of chronic renal failure (CRF) are poorly controlled diabetes, poorly controlled hypertension and chronic glomerulonephritis. Other causes 
include polycystic kidney disease, reflux nephropathy, kidney stones and prostate disease.3"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Ref.5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling "The product literature does not refer to renal failure or any other renal reaction1. There is a warning in the product information regarding renal impairment which indicates that no adjustment to the starting dose is needed for patients with mild or moderate renal impairment. There are only limited data available in patients with severe renal impairment. Vemurafenib should be used with caution in patients with severe renal impairment and patients should be closely monitored1. There are no reports in the literature of renal failure in association with vemurafenib. There is, however, an isolated report of renal failure in a patient taking vemurafenib. In a Phase II trial of the efficacy of the drug in 132 patients, one patient died owing to rapid  progression of  melanoma and ARF, possibly related to the study drug.4 A recent review of the on-target toxicities of TKIs noted that renal failure is an effect of the TKIs bosutinib, dasatinib, erlotinib, imatinib, nilotinib, sorafenib and sunitinib.5"^^xsd:string)

