DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_vemurafenib_and_renal_failure_3rd:MechanismContent "Vemurafenib is a low molecular weight, orally available, inhibitor of BRAF serine-threonine kinase (a member of the Raf kinase family of growth signal transduction protein kinases). Mutations in the BRAF gene which substitute the valine at amino acid position 600 result in constitutively activated BRAF proteins, which can cause cell proliferation in the absence of growth factors that would normally be required for proliferation.1 Before taking vemurafenib, patients must have BRAF V600 mutation-positive tumour status confirmed by a validated test. It is one of the medications widely known as tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) but should be referred to as protein kinase inhibitors because although the earliest drugs in this class were TKIs, they can now be further categorized based upon the amino acid that they phosphorylate:  serine,  threonine or tyrosine."^^xsd:string)

