# Individual: X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion (Discussion and conclusion)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion "Discussion and conclusion"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Conclusion X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Discussion X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:ref2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_dronedarone_and_polyneuropathy_4th:DiscussionAndConclusion "The spontaneous reports in VigiBase point overall towards a causal association between dronedarone and polyneuropathy despite confounders such as previous neurotoxic treatment in one case and co- medication with statins in two cases. The four reported dechallenges led to recovery and in the only case where drug continuation was reported, the patient did not recover from polyneuropathy. Dates for start of treatment and onset of polyneuropathy are incompletely reported.
Dronedarone is, however, the only drug suspected
by the reporters to have caused the ADR.
Dronedarone is structurally closely related to amiodarone, a drug for which neuropathy is a well- documented adverse reaction. While the addition of a methylsulfonamide group might have significantly reduced the neurotoxic potential of the drug as the results of the DIONYSOS study
seem to indicate,2 it cannot be safely assumed
that this structural change has completely eliminated neurotoxicity and a class effect seems therefore possible."^^xsd:string)

