# Individual: X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism (Agomelatine mechanism)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism "Agomelatine mechanism"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2014_m_agomelatine_and_thrombocytopenia_6th:AgomelatineMechanism "Agomelatine, the first melatonergic antidepressant, was designed to improve depressed states by resynchronizing perturbed biological rhythms. Its ‘synergistic’ agonist properties at melatonin receptors (MT1/MT2) plus antagonist properties at 5-hydroxytryptamine 2C (5-HT2C) receptors account for its beneficial influence on depressed states.1 The 5-HT2C receptor inhibits the release of norepinephrine and dopamine. By antagonizing the 5-HT2C receptor, agomelatine disrupts the previous inhibition effect, resulting in the release of norepinephrine and dopamine and increasing their extracellular levels.  Drugs that inhibit or induce the cytochrome P450 isoenzyme CYP1A2 have the potential to interact with agomelatine.2"^^xsd:string)

