# Individual: <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm> (Potential mechanism of finasteride triggering convulsions-the testosterone/progesterone paradigm)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm> "Potential mechanism of finasteride triggering convulsions-the testosterone/progesterone paradigm"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm>)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm> X2014_m_finasterine_and_convulsions_4th:convulsions)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm> X2014_m_finasterine_and_convulsions_4th:ref18)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#PotentialMechanismOfFinasterideTriggeringconvulsions-thetestosterone/progesteroneParadigm> "Additionally, progesterone acts as an anticonvulsant, while testosterone has pro-convulsant properties. An excess of testosterone brought about by finasteride treatment (through inhibition of the enzyme that converts it to 5a-DHT) results in a higher concentration of 17P-estradiol, a potent pro- convulsant hormone.18 "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#convulsions/epilepticSeizuresPathobiology> (convulsions/epileptic seizures pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#convulsions/epilepticSeizuresPathobiology> "convulsions/epileptic seizures pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#convulsions/epilepticSeizuresPathobiology>)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2014_4_finasterine_convulsions#convulsions/epilepticSeizuresPathobiology> "An epileptic seizure is defined as a transient occurrence of signs and/or symptoms due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity in the brain.

Seizures can be generalized and concerning activity in both hemispheres; or partial, concerning a localized group of neurons. Partial seizures can further be defined as complex if consciousness is altered during an episode and simple if they do not affect memory and awareness. Seizures that can be attributed to an agent or underlying condition are considered symptomatic; seizures for which no underlying factor can be identified are considered unprovoked and attributable to epilepsy.
Symptomatic seizures can be induced by a multitude of factors including: stroke, head injury, anoxia, hypoglycaemia, infections, fever, menstruation, drugs and non-adherence to antiepileptic drugs. The incidence of acute symptomatic seizures is 29-39 per 100 000 per
year, while the incidence of single unprovoked seizures is 23-61 per 100,000 person-years.4
The incidence of drug-induced seizures is difficult to determine.5 Although numerous drugs
can induce a convulsive state or lower the seizure threshold, the most common classes of drug are central acting agents, anti-diabetics, sympathomimetics and anti-asthmatics"^^xsd:string)

