# Individual: X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 (Clinical trials info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 "Clinical trials info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Clinical_trial_information X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:desloratadine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ClinicalTrial1 "In a clinical trial where up to 20 mg of desloratadine (four times the indicated dose for adults) was administered daily for two weeks, no cardiovascular effect was observed. In another trial, where desloratadine was administered at a dose of 45 mg daily (which is nine times the clinical dose) for ten days, no prolongation of QTc interval was observed.1 In three placebo-controlled clinical trials 246 paediatric subjects aged 6 months to 11 years received desloratadine as an oral solution for 15 days. There were no clinically meaningful changes in any electrocardiographic parameter, including the QTc interval.2"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ConclusionContent (Conclusion content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ConclusionContent "Conclusion content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Conclusion X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ConclusionContent)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:ConclusionContent "QT prolongation can have many alternative causes and can either be acquired from e.g. drugs or be congenital. Several of the cases in Vigibase® present alternative or contributory factors to the reaction but eight out of the nine unique cases report a positive dechallenge and three reports have a rapid time to onset recorded the same day as the drug was administered. Two reporters have also assessed the reactions as probably related to desloratadine. The fact that loratadine, the parent drug of desloratadine, has been reported to interact with other drugs, namely amiodarone which was also used as a long term treatment by one of the patients with a rapid time to onset in this case series, makes a possible hypothesis that also desloratadine could have this potential. The product label states that it is not known which enzyme is responsible for the metabolism of desloratadine and that interactions with other medicinal products cannot be excluded. This possibility is highlighted in this signal."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_desloratadine_and_qt_prolongation_2nd:Desloratadine_and_QT_prolongation (Desloratadine and QT prolongation)

