# Individual: X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis (metastatic melanoma and sepsis)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis "metastatic melanoma and sepsis"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastaticMelanoma)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:sepsis)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_sepsis_2nd:metastatic_melanoma_and_sepsis "Patients with metastatic malignant disease, such as metastatic melanoma, are at increased risk of sepsis: contributing factors include procedural interventions that breech epithelial barriers (e.g. surgical tumour removal; insertion of intravenous catheters for delivery of chemotherapeutic medicines), blockage of normally patent structures by metastatic tumours (e.g. urinary tract infection secondary to an obstructed ureter; cholangitis secondary to an obstructed gall bladder; pneumonia secondary to an obstructed bronchus) and increased exposure to pathogens during hospitalization. Other host factors that may contribute to sepsis in patients with metastatic malignant melanoma include diminished nutritional status and the use of medications that impair the immune response (e.g. corticosteroids; some chemotherapeutic agents)."^^xsd:string)

