# Individual: X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors (Potential AE mechanism through HT4 receptors)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors "Potential AE mechanism through HT4 receptors"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:suicidalIdeation)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Ref.11)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Ref.16)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_Prucalopride__3rd_2015:Potential_AE_mechanism_through_HT4_receptors "The 5-HT4 receptor (5-HT4-R) is located both in the CNS and in the peripheral tissues. In the human brain, 5-HT4-Rs have been localized in the basal ganglia, the hippocampal formation and the cortical mantle.3 It could be hypothesized that prucalopride, acting upon the 5-HT4 receptors in the basal ganglia could lead to a syndrome of dysphoria and suicidal ideation, as substantia nigra hyperactivity has been implicated in schizophrenia.11 Also, available evidence for another serotonin receptor agonist, metoclopromide, suggest that different polymorphisms in 5-HT4 receptor HTR4 genes are associated with adverse events and clinical effectiveness. There is the potential that only patients with certain genetic variations in the 5- HT4 receptor are susceptible to neuropsychiatric side effects.16"^^xsd:string)

