# Individual: X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia (Possible mechanism of vemurafenib inducing thrombocytopenia)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia "Possible mechanism of vemurafenib inducing thrombocytopenia"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:thrombocytopenia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:Ref.10)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:Ref.11)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:Ref.9)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_vemurafenib_and_thrombocytopenia_3rd:PossibleMechanismOfVemurafenibInducingThrombocytopenia "Vemurafenib acts on mutated B-RAF protein to inhibit the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK intracellular signalling pathway in melanoma cells to prevent cell growth and proliferation. This same pathway is also present in haematopoietic progenitor cells and plays a role in haematopoietic cell differentiation,9,10 suggesting a possible mechanism by which vemurafenib might cause thrombocytopenia. Platelets (thrombocytes) are formed from megakaryocytes, which derive from the multipotential hematopoietic stem cell (HSC). The HSC gives rise to progressively committed progenitor cells, including the common myeloid progenitor (CMP) and the megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor (MEP). MEPs in turn give rise to both megakaryocytic and erythroid cell lineages. Multiple transcription factors are involved in the differentiation of these MEPs to megakaryocytes, the most important of which is thrombopoietin (TPO). Binding of TPO to the TPO receptor on the MEP cell surface membrane activates the intracellular signaling protein Jak2, which in turn activates several intracellular signaling cascades, including the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK cascade.11"^^xsd:string)

