# Individual: X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia (Dystonia pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia "Dystonia"^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia "Dystonia pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:dystonia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Dystonia "Dystonia denotes abnormal movements that are slow or so sustained that they may appear as abnormal postures. These abnormal movements of groups of muscles or body segments include grimacing, torticollis, blepharospasm and limb torsions. Generally, they are absent during sleep and exacerbated by emotional stress or voluntary activity. Dystonia occurs as an occasional complication of treatment with neuroleptic and dopaminergic drugs and many others. Drug-induced dystonia may be early (onset within one week of commencement of treatment) or late (onset after several weeks, months or years of treatment). Late persistent dystonia is usually termed tardive dyskinesia.2 Dystonia is a preferred term in WHO-ART with a number of included terms including trismus and various spasms (facial, infantile, cervical, oropharyngeal, tongue).  "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:IntroductionContent "Atomoxetine is a relatively potent inhibitor of the presynaptic noradrenaline transporter, a moderate inhibitor of 5HT uptake, and a weak inhibitor of dopamine uptake with minimal affinity for the other noradrenergic receptors. Atomoxetine has moderate affinity for 5HT2 and GABAA receptors but poor affinity for most other receptors. It is indicated for the treatment of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) as defined by DSMIV criteria in children 6 years of age and older, adolescents and adults. The most frequent adverse reactions reported during clinical trials of atomoxetine in children and adolescents include gastrointestinal reactions, increased blood pressure and heart rate, decreased appetite, decreased weight and skin reactions. Common neuropsychiatric reactions reported included dizziness, mood swings, somnolence, insomnia, irritability and depression.1 Dystonia denotes abnormal movements that are slow or so sustained that they may appear as abnormal postures. These abnormal movements of groups of muscles or body segments include grimacing, torticollis, blepharospasm and limb torsions. Generally, they are absent during sleep and exacerbated by emotional stress or voluntary activity. Dystonia occurs as an occasional complication of treatment with neuroleptic and dopaminergic drugs and many others. Druginduced dystonia may be early (onset within one week of commencement of treatment) or late (onset after several weeks, months or years of treatment). Late persistent dystonia is usually termed tardive dyskinesia.2 Dystonia is a preferred term in WHO-ART with a number of included terms including trismus and various spasms (facial, infantile, cervical, oropharyngeal, tongue). "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Literature_and_Labelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Literature_and_Labelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Literature_and_Labelling X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Ref.1)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:Literature_and_Labelling "The product literature does not refer to dystonia
although it does mention that very common,
common or uncommonly reported neurological
reactions included headache, dizziness,
somnolence including sedation, insomnia, syncope
and tremor. Post-marketing adverse neurological
events reported very rarely include seizures,
paraesthesia in children and adolescents,
hypoaesthesia and tics.1 No reports of dystonia in
association atomoxetine could be found in the
literature."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_atomoxetine_and_pediatric_patients_4rth_2015:MechanismOfAtomoxetine (Mechanism of atomoxetine)

