# Individual: X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase (Reports in Vigibase)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase "Reports in Vigibase"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_Olanzapine_and_accidental_drug_intake_by_children_5th:Reports_in_Vigibase "As of March 2015 there were eight reports of miosis for children below the age of 6 years. The signs of miosis reflect the anticholinergic properties of olanzapine. An assessment of the reports revealed that the WHOART preferred terms (PT) accidental drug intake by child, accidental overdose, or medication error was co-reported in six out of the eight reports. Widening the search to the WHO-ART HLT medication error related problems revealed 20 reports for olanzapine within the age group excluding two suspected duplicates. More than half of those reports represented accidental drug intake (by child, accidental exposure to product or accidental overdose). The reports originated from Asia, Europe, North America and Oceania."^^xsd:string)

