ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:gastroenteritis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:stomatitis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:temozolomide)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LabellingOfTemozolomide "Temozolomide is indicated for use in children from three years of age and in adults. Oesophagitis is not labelled for the drug in adults or in children, neither in the UK SPC nor the FDA label, however, stomatitis, dysphagia and gastroenteritis are listed as adverse drug reactions.4,5"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling (Literature and labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling "Literature and labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.6)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:LiteratureAndLabelling "Twelve cases of oesophagitis (WHO-ART PT) in association with temozolomide were identified in the WHO Global ICSR database, VigiBase®, in October 2014. Based on age, sex, country, type of report and other features, one duplicate (case 1) and one triplicate (case 8) have been identified. Thus, there are nine primary cases containing the reaction of interest (Table 1); in one, the reaction was reported as “oesophageal pain”. Age and sex were known in five cases while two cases only reported patient gender (Table 1). Two cases concerned children, an 8-year-old female and a 10-year-old male, while three involved male adults aged 62, 67 and 69; two cases of unknown age were female, while two cases had no information on the patient. No age or sex patterns emerge. In all cases the reaction appeared after the intake of the drug. Two reported time to onset (19 days and 31 days); in none was the reaction reported alone. Temozolomide was the only suspected drug in three of the reports. The drug was withdrawn in four cases: the outcome was unknown for two cases (1, 8), recovery as concerns one (case 2, which was reported as positive dechallenge) and no recovery in another (case 3). There is no information on dechallenge in case 7, however the outcome was recovery. No cases mention positive rechallenge. All cases except one, case 3, were considered as serious. In two cases the patient died due to severe myelosuppressive reactions. In November 2014 VigiBase® was also queried for oesophagitis (WHO-ART) in association with dacarbazine, obtaining seven cases. Additionally, this combination was highlighted through a disproportionality analysis with an IC of 1.29 and IC025 of 0.03."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide (Mechanism of temozolomide)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide "Mechanism of temozolomide"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Mechanism_of_temozolomide "Temozolomide is an oral triazene alkylating agent that has been available since the early 2000s; coupled with radiotherapy it is the first-line treatment for glioblastoma, the most common primary brain cancer in adults, against which its efficacy has been proven.1,2 At physiological pH, temozolomide is converted to the monomethyl-triazene metabolite, MTIC, which exerts the main cytotoxic action by methylating DNA at a number of sites. Temozolomide shares this metabolite and structural similarities with anoth"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_temozolomide_oesophagitis_5th:Oesophagitis (Oesophagitis pathobiology)

