# Individual: X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_1_info (case adult 1 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_1_info "case adult 1 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_1_info)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_1_info "One case, reported by a specialist doctor, indicates a possible interaction effect from concomitant use of desloratadine and risperidone. The case describes a 38 year-old male who had used desloratadine for years when adding risperidone for autistic disorder. A few hours after the first dose of risperidone the patient experienced violent thoughts, difficulty in standing, dystonia, sedation, trismus and salivation. After withdrawal of risperidone the reactions abated. The patient had previously experienced violent thoughts while on another antipsychotic drug. The reactions may be explained by risperidone alone in this case, however in the light of the paediatric case 1, which also has risperidone co-reported, the interaction hypothesis may also be worth consideration."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_2_info (case adult 2 info)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_2_info "case adult 2 info"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_2_info)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:case_adult_2_info "The remaining report describes a 32 year-old male presenting with aggressiveness 1-1.5 hours after desloratadine intake. The patient had taken several doses and the reaction is reported to have occurred after each intake. This case also describes a positive dechallenge, with a recovery within 36 hours after drug withdrawal, as well as a positive rechallenge. No concomitant medication was reported. The patient had previously used loratadine and cetirizine without experiencing this reaction."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study (cerminara et al study)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study "cerminara et al study"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001197 X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:seizures)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:desloratadine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:Ref.16)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2015_m_Desloratadine_and_aggressive_reaction_6th:cerminara_et_al_study "Cerminara et al. described seizures induced by desloratadine in four children. They speculated that susceptible patients might have a mutation in the gene coding for P-gp, causing an abnormal variant of P-gp, and thus limiting the efflux of desloratadine from the CNS.16"^^xsd:string)

