DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport3 "Treated with metoprolol"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport4 (Case information for report 4)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport4 "Case information for report 4"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport4 "Carvedilol is a substrate and inhibitor of the efflux transporter P-glycoprotein and there is in vitro evidence that vemurafenib has the same characteristics.1 It is possible, in case 4, that carvedilol increased the bioavailability of vemurafenib and this is supported by the observation that the patient also developed fever with no evidence of infection, a documented adverse reaction to vemurafenib."^^xsd:string)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport4 "Possible interaction with carvedilol via P glycoprotein. Vemurafenib restarted after 15 days.** Treated with metoprolol and warfarin. ECG 7 days after vemurafenib re-started showed sinus rhythm"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport5 (Case information for report 5)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport5 "Case information for report 5"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Case_Report_Information X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport5 "Cardioversion Vemurafenib half-life of 59 hours may explain persistence for 4 days. Amiodarone inhibition of vemurafenib CYP3A4 metabolism possible."^^xsd:string)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:CaseInformationForReport5 "Case 5 is of interest as the patient had pre-existing atrial fibrillation treated with amiodarone. Both vemurafenib and amiodarone are substrates for CYP3A4. Amiodarone is an inhibitor and there is evidence that vemurafenib is an inducer.1 Theoretically therefore, atrial fibrillation may have recurred because amiodarone clearance was increased thus reducing its therapeutic effect and vemurafenib clearance reduced thus increasing its postulated pro-arrhythmic effect."^^xsd:string)

