# Individual: X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Possible_mechanism_of_vemurafenib_amiodarone_ADR (Possible mechanism of vemurafenib amiodarone ADR)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Possible_mechanism_of_vemurafenib_amiodarone_ADR "Possible mechanism of vemurafenib amiodarone ADR"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Possible_mechanism_of_vemurafenib_amiodarone_ADR)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Possible_mechanism_of_vemurafenib_amiodarone_ADR X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Ref.1)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_vemurafenib_and_atrial_fibrillation_1st_2016:Possible_mechanism_of_vemurafenib_amiodarone_ADR "Both vemurafenib and amiodarone are substrates for CYP3A4. Amiodarone is an inhibitor and there is evidence that vemurafenib is an inducer.1 Theoretically therefore, atrial fibrillation may have recurred because amiodarone clearance was increased thus reducing its therapeutic effect and vemurafenib clearance reduced thus increasing its postulated pro-arrhythmic effect. "^^xsd:string)

