# Individual: X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction (Introduction)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction "Introduction"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Introduction "Benznidazole is a nitroimidazole derivative indicated for the treatment of Chagas disease (American Trypanosomiasis), a tropical protozoan infection with Trypanosoma cruzi endemic in Central and South America. The drug shows good efficacy in the acute stage of the disease but limited therapeutic success in chronic disease. The acute stage often goes unnoticed as it manifests with unspecific symptoms, if any. In the chronic stage, less than half of the patients will develop life-threatening cardiomyopathy and digestive disease (megaoesophagus and megacolon).1 Drugs available for treatment are nifurtimox and benznidazole. Commercial preparations of benznidazole have been available in Central and Latin America since 2012, ending a long period of shortage after the first commercialized products were discontinued in 1998, more than 25 years after their appearance on the market. The product information leaflet of Abarax®, dated 2012, lists mostly benign cutaneous eruptions among possible side effects, with the recommendation to discontinue treatment if fever and/or adenopathy occur.2 Severe skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) or toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) are not listed. Several publications on the treatment of Chagas disease document rash as a frequent adverse reaction to benznidazole without any mention of SJS or TEN."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:LiteratureAndLabelling (Literature and labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:LiteratureAndLabelling "Literature and labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:LiteratureAndLabelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:LiteratureAndLabelling X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:LiteratureAndLabelling "A possible causal association for severe skin reactions and benznidazole is further supported by the fact that SJS and TEN have been associated with imidazole derivatives, and are listed in the Summary of Product Characteristics of metronidazole.3"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Mechanism_of_benznidazole (Mechanism of benznidazole)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Mechanism_of_benznidazole "Mechanism of benznidazole"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Mechanism_of_benznidazole)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2016_m_Benznidazole_and_severe_skin_reactions_2nd_2016:Mechanism_of_benznidazole "Benznidazole is a nitroimidazole derivative indicated for the treatment of Chagas disease (American Trypanosomiasis), a tropical protozoan infection with Trypanosoma cruzi endemic in Central and South America. The drug shows good efficacy in the acute stage of the disease but limited therapeutic success in chronic disease."^^xsd:string)

