# Individual: X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.6)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureAndLabelling "The most recent patient information leaflet for  levothyroxine products in the UK states that  levothyroxine may worsen existing heart  problems, such as fast or irregular heartbeats,  awareness of beating of the heart, and chest  pain. Nervousness, excitability, restlessness,  and difficulty sleeping are also mentioned as  possible adverse drug reactions. Beyond that,  anxiety and confusion are highlighted as the  adverse effects of accidentally taking too many  tablets at once. 'Panic attacks and disorders'  are not mentioned. 4 
Similarly, the drug label information directed to  consumers for a levothyroxine sodium tablet in the US lists headache, hyperactivity, nervousness,  anxiety, irritability, emotional lability and insomnia  as adverse drug reactions related to the central  nervous system; palpitations, tachycardia,  arrhythmias, increased pulse and blood pressure,  heart failure, angina, myocardial infarction and  cardiac arrest are mentioned as cardiovascular  adverse drug reactions. It does not explicitly  mention panic attacks and disorders. 5 A PubMed search for ‘levothyroxine panic’ returned  25 results but none that appeared to be related to a  possible causal association between levothyroxine  treatment and 'Panic attacks and disorders'. It did  highlight papers that have studied the relationship  between thyroid function and panic disorder, such  as that by Kikuchi et al, which found significant  correlation between thyroid hormone levels and the  severity of anxiety or panic attacks in non -medicated patients with panic disorder. 6 "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin (Literature for panic attacks and levothyroxin)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin "Literature for panic attacks and levothyroxin"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001125 X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:anxiety)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:panicAttack)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:levothyroxin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.6)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:LiteratureForPanicAttacksAndLevothyroxin "A PubMed search for ‘levothyroxine panic’ returned  25 results but none that appeared to be related to a  possible causal association between levothyroxine  treatment and 'Panic attacks and disorders'. It did  highlight papers that have studied the relationship  between thyroid function and panic disorder, such  as that by Kikuchi et al, which found significant  correlation between thyroid hormone levels and the  severity of anxiety or panic attacks in non -medicated patients with panic disorder "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Mechanism_of_levothyroxin (Mechanism of levothyroxin)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Mechanism_of_levothyroxin "Mechanism of levothyroxin"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Mechanism_of_levothyroxin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Mechanism_of_levothyroxin X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.1)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Mechanism_of_levothyroxin "Thyroxine is the hormone responsible for  maintaining the metabolic rate of the body.   Levothyroxine is a synthetic preparation of T4  L-thyroxine used for lifelong hormone replacement  therapy in patients with hypothyroidism."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases (Netherlands cases)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases "Netherlands cases"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001197 X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:overdose)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:panicAttack)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:levothyroxin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:Netherlands_cases "Many of the 30 cases from the Netherlands are  part of a larger case series where a change in  packaging from bottles to blister packs is suspected to have led to better preservation of  the active substance, causing higher effective  doses at the same nominal strength of the  tablets. 3 This would be consistent with the  hypothesis that the panic attacks were mainly  the result of a therapeutic over-dose  "^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:PanicAttackDefinition (Panic attack definition)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:PanicAttackDefinition "Panic attack definition"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:PanicAttackDefinition)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:PanicAttackDefinition X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:panicAttack)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_levothyroxin_2nd:PanicAttackDefinition "Panic attacks are brief periods with a sudden  onset of intense discomfort, anxiety or fear.  They are accompanied by somatic symptoms such as chest pain, dizziness, and palpitations;  and by cognitive symptoms such as fear of dying, fear of going crazy, and depersonalization. Up  to 10% of the population have been estimated  to suffer one or more panic attacks in a single  year . It may also work the other way around,  in which case a sudden onset of palpitations or  chest pain triggers anxiety in predisposed  patients"^^xsd:string)

