# Individual: X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction (introduction)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction "introduction"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction X2017_m_libido_5th:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction X2017_m_libido_5th:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_libido_5th:introduction "The MedDRA preferred term 'loss of libido' was initialy highlighted for two systemic hormonal contraceptives (desogestrel and ethinylestradiol/ levonorgestrel) in the joint UMC/Lareb signal detection sprint focusing on patient reports that took place in October 2016. Although the terms 'decreased libido', 'increased libido' or 'changes in libido' are covered in most summary of product characteristics and patient information leaflets for systemic hormonal contraceptives, a complete loss of libido as reported and described by these patients is rarely mentioned.1,2 Many health-care professionals may know that most systemic hormonal contraceptives are known to be able to affect the libido and in some cases cause a complete loss of libido. The full extent of this effect does not, however, always seem to be known among the women using these products. A decrease in libido, as listed in many of the patient information leaflets, may be acceptable to some patients, but a loss of libido as an adverse effect of contraceptive use would be less so."^^xsd:string)

