# Individual: X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology (Multiple sclerosis pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology "Multiple sclerosis pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:multipleSclerosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2017_m_natalizumab_6th:Multiple_sclerosis_pathophysiology "MS is an autoimmune chronic inflammatory condition, the pathogenesis of which is still to be fully characterized, but it is accompanied by a dysregulation of the blood-brain barrier leading to an increased permeability and trafficking of lymphocytes. Natalizumab has positive effects in MS, thanks to its VLA4-VCAM-1 complex inhibiting action. In healthy individuals, the vascular parenchyma of the blood–brain barrier restricts the access to the CNS, making it an immunoprivileged site. VCAM-1 is minimally expressed on endothelial blood brain barrier cells, but pro-inflammatory stimuli (such as tumour necrosis factors or interleukins) and chronic inflammatory conditions (such as MS) trigger its upregulation, facilitating lymphocyte entry into the CNS.3"^^xsd:string)

