# Individual: X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:interaction_between_enalapril_and_ciprofloxacin (interaction between enalapril and ciprofloxacin)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:interaction_between_enalapril_and_ciprofloxacin "interaction between enalapril and ciprofloxacin"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:interaction_between_enalapril_and_ciprofloxacin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:interaction_between_enalapril_and_ciprofloxacin X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:acuteKidneyInjury)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_ciprofloxacin_1st_2018:interaction_between_enalapril_and_ciprofloxacin "A potential mechanism for the observed interaction has not been identified. Most of the published reports summarised by Lomaestro indicate acute interstitial nephritis as the underlying cause of AKI with fluoroquinolones.2 This is rare although the estimated incidence of 1 in 1,526 in the case control study of hospital admissions is likely to be an underestimate as patients may have been admitted with an alternative 2,primary diagnosis or recovered before needing admission. Interstitial nephritis or other pathology due to a fluoroquinolone could be a trigger for ACE inhibitor-related AKI or more severe AKI leading to admission."^^xsd:string)

