AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_ivermectin_1st:Lethargy "lethargy"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect X2018_ivermectin_1st:Lethargy)

# Individual: X2018_ivermectin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_ivermectin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2018_ivermectin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_ivermectin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling X2018_ivermectin_1st:Ref.1)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_ivermectin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling "The neurological events of dizziness (2.8%), somnolence (0.9%), vertigo (0.9%), and tremor (0.9%) were observed in human clinical trials for the treatment of strongyloidiasis, while drug-related headache (0.2%) was observed in trials for onchocerciasis; these events are included in the product label. Also included in the labelling is a warning for overdose which can manifest with headache, dizziness, asthenia, seizure, ataxia, and paresthesia.1 The label for ivermectin also provides the following warning: “Rarely, patients with onchocerciasis who are also heavily infected with Loa loa may develop a serious or even fatal encephalopathy either spontaneously or following treatment with an effective microfilaricide. In these patients, the following adverse experiences have also been reported: pain (including neck and back pain), red eye, conjunctival hemorrhage, dyspnea, urinary and/or fecal incontinence, difficulty in standing/ walking, mental status changes, confusion, lethargy, stupor, seizures, or coma. This syndrome has been seen very rarely following the use of ivermectin. In individuals who warrant treatment with ivermectin for any reason and have had significant exposure to Loa loa-endemic areas of West or Central Africa, pre-treatment assessment for loiasis and careful post- treatment follow-up should be implemented.”.1"^^xsd:string)

