# Individual: X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology (Steven-Johnson syndrome pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology "Steven-Johnson syndrome pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Stevens-JohnsonSyndrome)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:ToxicEpidermalNecrolysis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Ref.6)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_artemether_2nd_2018:Steven-Johnson_syndrome_pathophysiology "Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) is a severe mucocutaneous immune reaction, characterized by necrosis and detachment of the epidermis of the skin through apoptosis of keratinocytes.3 Common prodromal symptoms of SJS are flu-like symptoms such as fever, sore throat, headache and fatigue, which may initially be misdiagnosed as an infection and treated as such. Typically, a maculopapular blistering skin rash abruptly arises on the face, torso and may spread to arms, legs and soles. Oral, ocular or genital mucous membranes are affected in a majority of patients. The most common cause of SJS is drugs, including anti-malarials. The condition may also be triggered by malignant disease and infections, and regarding the latter, protozoal infections such as malaria and trichomoniasis have been described as causal factors.4 In a significant proportion of the cases no clear causal factor can be identified. Genetic factors such as being a slow acetylator may predispose to SJS. Stevens-Johnson syndrome and the closely related syndrome of toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) are variants of a disease continuum where in SJS skin detachment is <10% of the body surface area; in SJS/TEN overlap the area is 10-30% and in TEN involves detachment of >30% of the body surface.5,6 Onset is described as 1- 30 days after start of instigating factor."^^xsd:string)

