# Individual: X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Ref.6)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_vemurafenib_3rd:Literature_and_Labelling "The product literature does not refer to cardiac failure. Currently, the only cardiac events mentioned in the ADR section of the product labels are QT prolongation (EU SmPC) and atrial fibrillation (US FDA label).1,4 The EU SmPC does mention that “older patients (≥ 65 years) may be more likely to experience adverse reactions, including cuSCC, decreased appetite, and cardiac disorders.”1 There are no reports in the literature of cardiac failure in association with vemurafenib. There is a report of QT prolongation in association with treatment with vemurafenib in end stage renal disease although the authors assessed that the probability of this being an adverse drug reaction was low.5 The product information for another BRAF enzyme inhibitor, dabrafenib, has a warning that dabrafenib in combination with trametinib has been reported to decrease left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF).6 It also lists ejection fraction decreased as a common adverse reaction but this adverse reaction arises from combination with trametinib and not dabrafenib alone."^^xsd:string)

