# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis (Deep Vein Thrombosis pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis "Deep Vein Thrombosis pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:DeepVeinThrombosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Deep_Vein_Thrombosis "\"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is the formation of
a blood clot, or thrombus, in one of the deep veins
of the body, most commonly in veins of the lower
extremities. The mechanism of thrombus
formation typically involves a combination of
decreased blood flow, injury to the blood vessel
wall (endothelium), and an increased tendency to
clot. The annual incidence of DVT in the USA has
been estimated to be 80 cases per 100,000. Of the
more than 200,000 people that develop venous
thrombosis in that country each year, 50,000
cases are complicated by pulmonary embolism.3
Deep venous thrombosis usually affects individuals
older than 40 years, with increasing incidence with
age.\""^^xsd:string)

