# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept (Posology and Pharmacokinetics of Aflibercept)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept "Posology and Pharmacokinetics of Aflibercept"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Posology_and_Pharmacokinetics_of_Aflibercept "In the ocular indications, aflibercept is administered intravitreally as a 2 mg dose, to be repeated every month for three consecutive months, followed by every other month . In the cancer indications, it is administered intravenously 4 mg per kg of body weight prior to each FOLFIRI treatment cycle (every 2 weeks) .1,2 Free aflibercept is primarily cleared by binding to endogenous VEGF to form a stable, inactive complex. Free aflibercept exhibits a faster (nonlinear) clearance at doses below 2 mg/kg, likely due to the high affinity binding of aflibercept to endogenous VEGF. At higher doses, linear clearance is observed, probably due to nonsaturable biological mechanisms of elimination such as protein catabolism; at such doses, free aflibercept clearance was measured to be approximately 1.0 L/day with a terminal half-life of six days.2"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events (Possible mechanism behind VEGF inhibitors involvement  in thromboembolic events)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events "Possible mechanism behind VEGF inhibitors involvement  in thromboembolic events"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_class X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2018_6_aflibercept_deep_vein_thrombosis_pulmonary_embolism.owl#VEGF/VEGFR_(Vascular_Endothelial_Growth_Factor)_inhibitors>)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.6)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.7)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.8)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Possible_mechanism_behind_VEGF_invovlement_in_thromboembolic_events "The mechanism for thromboembolic events by VEGF inhibitors is probably multifactorial, given the number of effects VEGF has on vascular walls and the coagulation system.4 Inhibition of circulating VEGF may diminish the ability of the endothelial cells in the vascular wall to regenerate, thereby allowing any defects that expose procoagulant phospholipids on the luminal plasma membrane or underlying matrix to lead to thrombosis or haemorrhage.5 Furthermore, inhibition of the VEGF decreases production of NO and prostacyclin (PGI2, prostaglandin I2) which can predispose to thromboembolic events.6 Finally, an overproduction of erythropoietin caused by vascular endothelial growth factor inhibition may also increase risk of thrombosis by increasing haematocrit and blood viscosity."^^xsd:string)

