# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses (review of published meta-analyses)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses "review of published meta-analyses"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001197 X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Arterial_thrombosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:DeepVeinThrombosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_class X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2018_6_aflibercept_deep_vein_thrombosis_pulmonary_embolism.owl#VEGF/VEGFR_(Vascular_Endothelial_Growth_Factor)_inhibitors>)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.24)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:review_of_published_meta-analyses "As further support to this hypothesis, the authors of Prescrire recently issued a review of published meta-analyses and concluded that there is probably a two-fold increased risk of arterial or venous thrombosis with use of intravitreal anti-VEGF administration.24"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies (Studies examining VEGF serum levels following intravitreal injection)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies "Studies examining VEGF serum levels following intravitreal injection"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001197 X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_class X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies <http://purl.org/OpenPVSignal/Signals/2018_6_aflibercept_deep_vein_thrombosis_pulmonary_embolism.owl#VEGF/VEGFR_(Vascular_Endothelial_Growth_Factor)_inhibitors>)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.17)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.18)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.19)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:Ref.20)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:studies "Studies have examined VEGF serum levels following intravitreal anti-VEGF injection and have reported longer duration and more VEGF suppression in the serum after aflibercept compared to another anti-VEGF agent, ranibizumab.17-19 A more recent publication using radiolabelled anti-VEGF agents in non-human primates to investigate intravitreal pharmacokinetic properties and systemic biodistribution revealed aflibercept to have an intravitreal half-life of 2.44 days and to be detectable throughout the body until day 21"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:summary (summary)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:summary "summary"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Summary X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:summary)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_Aflibercept_6th_2018:summary "A novel approach to signal detection screening was recently investigated at UMC. Designed to be sensitive to disproportionality between subpopulations, the screening highlighted a significant increased reporting of deep vein thrombosis with the use of aflibercept in males compared to females. Aflibercept is an antagonist of VEGF (vascular endothelial growth factor) receptors and through this mechanism inhibits angiogenesis or the growth of new blood vessels. Aflibercept is used in the treatment of a number of ocular conditions with an ocular preparation called Eylea and in the treatment of metastatic colorectal cancer with an intravenous preparation called Zaltrap. Clinical assessment of the combination of aflibercept and deep vein thrombosis included review of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism in both indications of use for aflibercept as well as a closer investigation of potential gender related factors which could support a hypothesis of an increased risk for these events in men."^^xsd:string)

