AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Brivudine "Mechanism of Brivudine"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Brivudine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Brivudine X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Brivudine X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Brivudine "Brivudine is a nucleoside analogue used in the treatment of herpes zoster, for which it is approved in a number of EU countries. Brivudine owes its effect to preferential phosphorylation by VZV encoded tyrosine kinase (TK) to its 5'-diphosphate form. After further conversion to triphosphate form, it acts as an inhibitor or alternate substrate for the viral DNA polymerase. It has a terminal half-life of 16 hours, and is degraded to its base form, bromovinyl uracil (BVU), by thymidine phosphorylase"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate (Mechanism of Uridine Triacetate)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate "Mechanism of Uridine Triacetate"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Ref.23)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_Uridine_Triacetate "Uridine triacetate is an acetylated form of uridine; following oral administration, it is deacetylated, and uridine competitively inhibits cell damage and death by fluorouracil."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction (Mechanism of fatal drug interaction)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction "Mechanism of fatal drug interaction"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2018_m_brivudine_5fu_6th_2018:Mechanism_of_fatal_drug_interaction "The mechanism of action for this fatal drug interaction is the same for brivudine; bromovinyl uracil, the major metabolite of both drugs, irreversibly binds to and inhibits dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD). DPD is an enzyme in the hepatic system responsible for the inactivation of 5-FU.4 An important additional fact is that DPD is responsible for about 85% of 5-FU metabolism. Its activity is immediately and almost completely suppressed, with the introduction of BVU, and toxicity-averting DPD activity did not return until at least 14 days after the cessation of SRV. A four-week period between the use of 5-FU and brivudine 
or other BVU producing substances should be enough to avoid 5-FU toxicity.7"^^xsd:string)

