# Individual: X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9 (Polymorphic CYP2C9)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9 "Polymorphic CYP2C9"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.12)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.13)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Polymorphic_CYP2C9 "The polymorphic CYP2C9 isoenzyme catalyses the biotransformation of SUs in the liver. The mutant alleles CYP2C9*2 and CYP2C9*3 are known to have a reduced drug-metabolizing activity than the wildtype CYP2C9*1, the decrease in catalysing activity of the *3 allele being more pronounced. The
*3 variant is most common in Asians with a frequency of 10%–25% compared to that of 2%– 6% in Caucasians. Involvement of CYP2C19 in the metabolism of SUs is also reported. CYP2C19*2 and CYP2C19*3 are variants that encode a non- functional CYP2C19 enzyme. Individuals with either of the variants are labelled as poor metabolizers.12
In Asian populations, genetic variability of CYP2C9 is dominated by the less functioning allele *3 (3.4% in East Asians and 11.3% in South Asians) while in Europeans, genetic variability expresses mainly the
*2 variant (Europeans 11.7% and admixed Americans 6.6%), Other allelic variants are also present in South Asian and African populations."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 (Possible drug drug interactions 2)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 "Possible drug drug interactions 2"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Hypoglycemia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.23)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.24)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.25)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.26)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_drug_drug_interactions_2 "Interactions: Several published cases report hypoglycaemia induced by hydroxychloroquine, and observational studies suggest a dose-dependent protective effect of hydroxychloroquine on drug- induced diabetes in rheumatic patients treated with corticosteroids.23-26 Co-administration of anti- infective agents that are CYP2C9 inhibitors can increase the risk of hypoglycaemia in glipizide and glyburide users.26 Therefore, co-administration of other CYP2C9 inhibitors might also increase the risk of hypoglycaemia, although an increased risk of hypoglycaemia might not be present with less strong (non-clinically-relevant) CYP2C9 inhibitors. P-Glycoprotein inhibitors might also increase the risk of hypoglycaemia.26 A few ICSRs of this set mentioned suspected or concomitant drugs that could interact with glibenclamide. It is worth noting that an interacting agent can theoretically exert additive effects on an isoenzyme with genetically reduced functionality."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_interactions (Possible drug drug interactions 1)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_interactions "Possible drug drug interactions 1"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001182 X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_interactions)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Possible_interactions "An ICSR from a non-Asian country reports hypoglycaemia in a 78-year-old woman treated with hydroxychloroquine because of a rheumatoid arthritis. Three Asian country reports mention drugs possibly interacting: one of a 75- year-old woman, who started a triple therapy with metformin 1 g, pioglitazone 30 mg and glibenclamide 5 mg, and presented with palpitations and dizziness on the seventh day; another one mentions phenytoin administration with confusing TTO dates; another report mentions hypoglycaemic symptoms the same day that amoxicillin was administered, and 10 days after the therapy with glibenclamide was started."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Prescription_of_glibenclamide (Prescription of glibenclamide)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Prescription_of_glibenclamide "Prescription of glibenclamide"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Prescription_of_glibenclamide)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Prescription_of_glibenclamide X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Ref.11)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Glibenclamideglyburide_and_palpitations_in_the_Asian_population_2nd_2019:Prescription_of_glibenclamide "According to the National List of Essential Medicines (NLEMs), glibenclamide (2.5 and 5.0 mg) is an essential SU in five countries, India, Pakistan, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Bangladesh. In Sri Lanka, glibenclamide is the most commonly prescribed SU by GPs.11"^^xsd:string)

