ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:CNSStimulation)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Encephalopathy)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:PeripheralNeuropathy)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Seizures)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:convulsions)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:intracranialPressure)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:phychosis)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_class X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:fluoroquinolones)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Levofloxacin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:LevofloxacinAndCNSReactions "The US FDA label for levofloxacin1 lists convulsions and toxic psychoses as the major CNS reactions reported with fluoroquinolones in clinical trials. A number of other psychiatric and nervous system reactions are listed. These have not been confined to the elderly or those with predisposing conditions. Fluoroquinolones may also increase intracranial pressure and cause CNS stimulation. Peripheral neuropathy has been reported rarely and isolated cases of encephalopathy have been observed since marketing. Myoclonus may therefore be an outcome of other levofloxacin adverse reactions such as seizure activity, encephalopathy and peripheral neuropathy. The elderly are noted to be at increased risk of certain fluoroquinolone-related disorders but these do not include CNS adverse effects. There is a warning that, as with other fluoroquinolones, levofloxacin should be used with caution in patients with a known or suspected CNS disorder that may predispose them to seizures or lower the seizure threshold (e.g., severe cerebral arteriosclerosis, epilepsy) or in the presence of other risk factors that may predispose them to seizures or lower the seizure threshold (e.g., certain drug therapy, renal dysfunction). Recommended dose adjustments for renal failure are provided to avoid levofloxacin accumulation. The UK SmPC for levofloxacin contains similar information.2"^^xsd:string)

