# Individual: X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin (Pharmacokinetics of levofloxacin)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin "Pharmacokinetics of levofloxacin"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Ref.4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_levofloxacin_1st:Pharmacokinetics_of_levofloxacin "The preferred route of administration is oral, showing a rapid and almost complete absorption after 1-2 hours and 99-100% bioavailability. It is excreted primarily as unchanged drug in the urine within 48 hours of administration, by tubular secretion in addition to glomerular filtration. In patients with impaired renal function (creatinine clearance < 50 mL/min), adjustment of the dose regimen, relative to the recommended dose for the indication, is necessary to avoid levofloxacin accumulation.1 Levofloxacin has been shown to penetrate a range of peripheral tissues1 but it is believed that its penetration into cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is poor. In vitro and in vivo studies have suggested that the brain distribution of several quinolones is restricted by the operation of multiple efflux transporters"^^xsd:string)

