# Individual: X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology (Haematospermia pathophysiology)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology "Haematospermia pathophysiology"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:introduction)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:haematospermia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Ref.4)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haematospermia_pathology "Haematospermia is the presence of blood in the ejaculate. Haematospermia in itself is not harmful and usually has a benign origin. Although it is often harmless, it is frightening and concerning for patients. A malignant cause of haematospermia is rare and occurs almost exclusively in men aged over 40 years. The commonest causes are prostate biopsy, post-radiation treatment for prostate cancer and prostatitis. Other causes that have been identified include benign and malignant disorders of the urogenital tract, urogenital infections including schistosomiasis and those that are sexually transmitted, vascular malformations, and congenital or drug-induced bleeding disorders. However, in many cases a cause is not found and the condition resolves spontaneously.
Recommended investigations depend on the
persistence of haematospermia, associated symptoms and the age of the patient.3,4"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors (Mechanism of haemorrhage and factor Xa inhibitors)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors "Mechanism of haemorrhage and factor Xa inhibitors"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:hemorrhage)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_xa_factor_2019_3rd:Haemorrhage_and_factor_Xa_inhibitors "Factor Xa is required to activate prothrombin (Factor II) to thrombin (Factor IIa) which ultimately leads to fibrin clot formation and activation of platelets by thrombin. Factor Xa inhibitors therefore reduce clot formation.1,2 Directly linked to their mechanism of action is the risk of haemorrhages"^^xsd:string)

