ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:nausea)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:vomiting)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_drug X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:guaifenesin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Connection_of_guaifenesin_with_GI_adverse_effects "Guaifenesin has been described as inducing gastrointestinal discomfort, nausea and vomiting,2  and these reactions are listed in connection to guaifenesin in the SmPCs for these combination products.1"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content (Discussion content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content "Discussion content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Discussion X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.7)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.8)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.9)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Discussion_content "It is uncertain which of the substances in the combination product most likely caused the sudden abdominal pain. Guaifenesin has been described as inducing gastrointestinal discomfort, nausea and vomiting,2 and these reactions are listed in connection to guaifenesin in the SmPCs for these combination products.1 It is unclear why guaifenesin would induce sudden gastric pain in some individuals, but it does not seem unreasonable because of its stimulation of mucosa receptors in the gastrointestinal tract.7 Paracetamol overdose can cause abdominal pain as a first sign of liver damage, but in none of the 29 VigiBase cases was an over dosage suggested, however, eight patients used paracetamol concomitantly with the combination product. None of these cases included information on dosages for these co-reported products, so a possibility of overdose cannot be excluded. Phenylephrine hydrochloride acts with alpha-1 receptor stimulation, resulting in vasoconstriction in vessels of the skin, abdominal viscera and kidney, and theoretically abdominal pain could result from a decreased blood flow to the gastrointestinal tract.8 Only a few reports of abdominal pain have been received in VigiBase for phenylephrine as a single substance. One case in the literature describes a 70-year-old woman with henylephrine-associated ischaemic colitis presenting with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, and acute onset of haematochezia following ‘a lot’ of aspirin and phenylephrine use for sinusitis and nasal congestion.9 This case is interesting in the light of the 71-year-old male in the VigiBase case series, who experienced gastrointestinal polyp haemorrhage together with upper abdominal pain following use of the combination product. Further investigation of more serious consequences of phenylephrine use may be warranted. In a study investigating the bioavailability of a new oral syrup product containing guaifenesin, paracetamol, and phenylephrine, 45 patients were enrolled. Two patients reported GI adverse events (nothing more was specified) following the use of the test product consisting of the new oral syrup, and four patients reported GI adverse events following the use of the reference product consisting of an established oral liquid containing the same combination of substances. The authors considered the reported GI adverse events in this study as related to the liquid formulation, without any further discussion.10 In the VigiBase case series, the drug formulations used by the patients were liquid, tablet, capsule, and powder for oral solution, but patients in this case series still experienced sudden, severe abdominal pain in temporal association with these products. There is a possibility that some of the excipients in the products used by the patients in the VigiBase case series would cause abdominal pain. Sorbitol has been reported to be associated with abdominal pain, bloating and diarrhoea11, and was listed as the cause for abdominal pain in the label for one of the Swedish guaifenesin products.12 However, the products used by the patients in the VigiBase series did not always include sorbitol, so this excipient would not be the contender in many of the cases included in this signal. An obvious confounding factor is the underlying disease in these cases, where abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting could occur. However, for many cases, the upper abdominal pain had a sudden onset after drug intake, and there was recovery reported after stopping the drug, suggesting a causal relationship to the combination product."^^xsd:string)

