# Individual: X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Introduction_content "Combination products containing guaifenesin, paracetamol, and phenylephrine are approved for symptoms associated with colds, flu, headaches, blocked nose, sore throat, chills, fever, and chesty coughs.1 These products are marketed worldwide and are mostly sold over-the-counter.2 There are several brands on the market with various dosage strengths and formulations, e.g., liquid, tablet, capsule and powder for oral solutions. In the United Kingdom (UK), the strengths are either 100 or 200 mg for guaifenesin; 250, 500, 1000 mg for paracetamol; and 5, 6.1, 10, 12.2 mg for phenylephrine hydrochloride.1 In the United States (US), there is a range of products with other formulations and strengths.3 Products are approved from the age of 12, or of 16 in the UK1, and from six years of age in the US.3 The MedDRA (Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities) preferred terms (PT) ‘abdominal pain’ and ‘abdominal pain upper’ include long lists of lowest level terms, such as stomach ache, stomach cramps or stomach pain but these terms in themselves do not cover the severity of the adverse reaction."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling (Literature and Labelling)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling "Literature and Labelling"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Literature_information X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.6)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Literature_and_Labelling "The Summary of Product Characteristics (SmPC) for combination products with guaifenesin, paracetamol, and phenylephrine in the UK list abdominal discomfort, nausea and vomiting in relation to guaifenesin; nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea in relation to phenylephrine; and pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia and abdominal pain in the overdose section for paracetamol.1 In some of the Patient Information Leaflets (PILs), the only gastrointestinal related adverse reactions listed are nausea and vomiting, and where abdominal discomfort is mentioned, it is expressed as abdominal/stomach discomfort or stomach aches/upsets.1 Combination products with these substances in the US list upset stomach or stomach pain as signs of liver problems.6"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_guaifenesin (Mechanism of guaifenesin)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_guaifenesin "Mechanism of guaifenesin"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_guaifenesin)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_guaifenesin X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_guaifenesin "It is unclear why guaifenesin would induce sudden gastric pain in some individuals, but it does not seem unreasonable because of its stimulation of mucosa receptors in the gastrointestinal tract."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_phenylephrine (Mechanism of phenylephrine)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_phenylephrine "Mechanism of phenylephrine"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_phenylephrine)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_phenylephrine X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Ref.8)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Gi_pain_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_phenylephrine "Phenylephrine hydrochloride acts with alpha-1 receptor stimulation, resulting in vasoconstriction in vessels of the skin, abdominal viscera and kidney, and theoretically abdominal pain could result from a decreased blood flow to the gastrointestinal tract."^^xsd:string)

