# Individual: X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content (Introduction content)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content "Introduction content"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Introduction X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.3)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.4)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.5)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.6)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.7)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Introduction_content "In April 2018, during a screening of VigiBase, the WHO global database of individual case safety reports, focusing on patient reports, the combination of methylphenidate and muscle tightness was identified as needing further in-depth assessment. Several cases described jaw tightness, and further exploration of VigiBase revealed many cases of trismus (lockjaw).
Methylphenidate is a centrally acting sympathomimetic indicated for the treatment of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD).1 The US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) first approved methylphenidate back in 1955, for various psychological disorders, and in the 1970s it was introduced to treat ADHD.2 Even though the drug has been on the market for a long time, the mechanism by which it exerts its therapeutic effect is still not clearly established. It is thought to facilitate the action of dopamine and norepinephrine through three mechanisms: 1) inhibition of reuptake, 2) facilitation of release into the synaptic cleft, and, 3) inhibition of the catabolic activity of monoamine oxidase.3
 
Trismus, or lockjaw, is the inability to open the mouth, usually because of muscle spasms. Causes include physical trauma, oral/dental surgery, infections, and temporomandibular joint disease (e.g. rheumatoid arthritis). Trismus can also be part of a drug-induced dystonic reaction. Antipsychotics (e.g. risperidone, aripiprazole) and antiemetics (e.g. metoclopramide) are known to cause dystonia, and it seems to be caused by an abnormal dopaminergic activity involving the basal ganglia.4
Dystonia is characterized by “muscular contractions or spasms that result in abnormal fixed postures or positions of the jaw, neck, shoulders, trunk, and extremities”.5 It can also result in twisting and repetitive movements. The symptoms are associated with pain and distress, and can cause difficulty with walking, speech, head turning, and swallowing. Dyskinesia, on the other hand is characterized by abnormal, involuntary, repetitive and persistent movements affecting the mouth and face, extremities and the trunk.6 Tardive dyskinesia can develop after treatment with dopamine receptor-blocking agents, usually one month after starting treatment.5 Symptoms are not painful but can still be debilitating.
Trismus, or any other jaw-related event, is not labelled for methylphenidate, but muscle tightness, muscle spasms, muscle twitching and muscle cramps are described.1,7 The labelled events are general and may not be specific enough for the patient to be able to connect them to trismus.
Hence, the possible association of methylphenidate and trismus was investigated further."^^xsd:string)

