# Individual: X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate (Mechanism of Methylphenydate)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate "Mechanism of Methylphenydate"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Mechanism X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.1)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.2)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.3)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_Methylphenydate "Methylphenidate is a centrally acting sympathomimetic indicated for the treatment of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD). The US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) first approved methylphenidate back in 1955, for various psychological disorders, and in the 1970s it was introduced to treat ADHD.2 Even though the drug has been on the market for a long time, the mechanism by which it exerts its therapeutic effect is still not clearly established. It is thought to facilitate the action of dopamine and norepinephrine through three mechanisms: 1) inhibition of reuptake, 2) facilitation of release into the synaptic cleft, and, 3) inhibition of the catabolic activity of monoamine oxidase."^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia (Mechanism of dystonia)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia "Mechanism of dystonia"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Adverse_Effect_Mechanism X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:dystonia)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Ref.5)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Methylphenidate_2019_4rth:Mechanism_of_dystonia "The mechanism behind dystonia is unclear but it seems to be caused by an abnormal dopaminergic activity involving the basal ganglia. Two contrasting hypotheses have been proposed: 1) dopaminergic hypofunction within basal ganglia leading to overactivity of the cholinergic system, and 2) hyperactivity of dopaminergic pathways.5 Therefore, any drug that alters dopamine signalling, whether by inhibiting it (such as for antipsychotics), or by increasing it, could in theory cause dystonic reactions. So, it could be hypothesized that methylphenidate can cause dystonic reactions such as trismus, through its stimulatory effect on dopamine signalling (inhibits reuptake and facilitates release of monoamines into synaptic cleft)."^^xsd:string)

