# Individual: X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf (Study of pathophysiologic mechanism of diarrhoea)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf "Study of pathophysiologic mechanism of diarrhoea"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(obo:OAE_0001125 X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:diarrhoea)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:Ref.14)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:dsf "A small study of 10 patients aimed to examine the underlying pathophysiologic 
mechanism of diarrhoea. Sigmoidoscopy revealed no evidence of ischaemia in any of the 10 subjects; however, gastroduodenoscopy revealed mucosal abnormalities in 8 patients.14"^^xsd:string)

# Individual: X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd (pathophysiology of ischaemic colitis)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd "pathophysiology of ischaemic colitis"^^xsd:string)
ClassAssertion(OpenPVSignal:Free_text_reporting_element X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:is_part_of X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:Introduction_content)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:refers_to_adverse_effect X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:colitis_ischaemic)
ObjectPropertyAssertion(mp:references X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:Ref.2)
DataPropertyAssertion(OpenPVSignal:has_content X2019_m_Nintedanib_and_ischaemic_colitis_6th_2019:fd "Ischaemic colitis is a condition caused by insufficient blood flow to the large intestine which results in mucosal ulceration, inflammation and haemorrhage. Causes can be either physiological (hypotension, secondary to embolus/thrombosis) or iatrogenic (secondary to medicines, surgery). Ischaemic colitis clinically manifests with diarrhoea, colicky abdominal pain, and rectal bleeding. It can be difficult for clinicians to differentiate ischaemic colitis from infective and inflammatory colitis. With its high mortality rate, patients with ischaemic colitis should be recognised quickly, as colonoscopic evaluation is recommended within 48 hours of symptom onset. Colonoscopy can distinguish those cases that may be treated with conservative management from those that require emergency resection. The complications which can arise from ischaemic colitis include obstruction, necrosis, and perforation.2"^^xsd:string)

